-- title:
--   Lam–Litt (3)$\Rightarrow$(2), normal form: integral derivatives at $0$, no $\omega$
-- statement:
--   Let $n\ge 0$, let $g\in\mathbb{Q}(z,y_0,\dots,y_{n-1})$ be a rational function in $n+1$ variables, and let $f=\sum_{k\ge 0}a_kz^k\in\mathbb{Q}[[z]]$ be a formal power series that solves the algebraic differential equation
--
--   $$f^{(n)}(z)=g\bigl(z,f(z),f'(z),\dots,f^{(n-1)}(z)\bigr)$$
--
--   at a non-singular point, meaning that $g$ is defined at $\bigl(0,f(0),f'(0),\dots,f^{(n-1)}(0)\bigr)$.
--
--   Assume in addition:
--
--   1. (Integral derivatives.) $k!\,a_k=f^{(k)}(0)\in\mathbb{Z}$ for all $k\ge 0$. In other words, $f=\sum_{k\ge 0}b_k\,z^k/k!$ is the exponential generating function of a sequence of integers $b_k$.
--   2. (Condition (3) of the Lam–Litt conjecture, in its form without $\omega$.) For every integer $m\ge 0$ there is $P_0$ such that for every prime $p\ge P_0$ the coefficients $a_0,a_1,\dots,a_{mp-1}$ have denominators prime to $p$.
--
--   Then $f$ has bounded denominators:
--
--   $$\exists N\ \ \forall k\ge 0:\quad a_k\in\mathbb{Z}[1/N].$$
--
--   This is an open problem. It is implication (3)$\Rightarrow$(2) of the Lam–Litt conjecture restricted to solutions all of whose derivatives at the origin are integers, and it is equivalent to the general implication. Indeed, for an arbitrary solution $f$ there is an integer $D\ge 1$ with $D^{k+1}k!\,a_k\in\mathbb{Z}$ for all $k$; the series $F(z)=D\,f(Dz)$ then has integral derivatives at the origin, solves a non-singular algebraic differential equation of the same order, and satisfies conditions (2) and (3) if and only if $f$ does. Hypothesis 2 is equivalent to the existence of a function $\omega$ on the primes with $\omega(p)/p\to\infty$ for which the coefficients are $\omega$-integral. In this normal form no prime is exceptional for the differential equation: for every prime $p$, the only possible source of $p$ in the denominator of $a_k=b_k/k!$ is the factorial.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The hypothesis on $f$ and $g$ is the mission's predicate `IsSolutionOfAlgebraicODE` ($g=p/q$ with $q$ non-zero at the initial point and $f^{(n)}q(z,f,\dots)=p(z,f,\dots)$); $n=0$ is allowed. Hypothesis 1 is stated as: every $k!\,a_k$ is the image of an integer. In hypothesis 2, $m$ ranges over all natural numbers and the condition on $a_k$ is that its reduced denominator is coprime to $p$, for $k<mp$. The conclusion uses the mission's `IsCoeffIntegralAdjointInvNat`, where $\mathbb{Z}[1/N]$ is the subalgebra of $\mathbb{Q}$ generated by $1/N$ (equal to $\mathbb{Z}$ for $N=0$).
-- source:
--   Normal form of implication (3) => (2) of Conjecture 1.1.1 of Y. H. J. Lam, D. Litt, Algebraicity and integrality of solutions to differential equations, https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.13175 (v1), p. 3 (mission milestone LamLitt.omega_integrality_implies_integrality; Formal Conjectures, FormalConjectures/LittProblems/1.lean, lam_litt.variants.omega_integrality_implies_algebraicity, https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures ). Hypothesis 2 is the description of condition (3) given on p. 3 right after the conjecture ('for all m > 0 and sufficiently large p (in terms of m), the first mp coefficients of the Taylor expansion of f have denominators which are prime to p'). The normalization to integral derivatives at the origin is not in the paper; it is equivalent to the general case by the rescaling z -> Dz (theorems LamLitt.factorial_mul_coeff_denominator_bound and LamLitt.isSolutionOfAlgebraicODE_smul_rescale on this platform).

import Definitions.Def_LamLitt_Defs
import Mathlib

open PowerSeries

namespace LamLitt

theorem omega_integrality_implies_integrality_hurwitz {n : ℕ} (f : PowerSeries ℚ)
    (g : MvRatFunc (Fin (n + 1)) ℚ) (hODE : IsSolutionOfAlgebraicODE n f g)
    (hH : ∀ k : ℕ, ∃ m : ℤ, (k.factorial : ℚ) * PowerSeries.coeff k f = (m : ℚ))
    (h3 : ∀ m : ℕ, ∃ P₀ : ℕ, ∀ p : Nat.Primes, P₀ ≤ (p : ℕ) →
      ∀ k : ℕ, k < m * (p : ℕ) → Nat.Coprime (PowerSeries.coeff k f).den p) :
    ∃ N : ℕ, IsCoeffIntegralAdjointInvNat f N := by sorry

end LamLitt
